WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.960
It's hard to believe one of the biggest television
franchises tried to hide its name.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.231
For one reason or another, all these shows
changed titles — some for the better, but

00:00:09.231 --> 00:00:11.610
others … not so much.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:16.129
It's easy to understand why Seinfeld is seen
today as one of the most popular, beloved,

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:18.510
and influential shows of the past 50 years.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:23.349
Its unique brand of fast-paced, whip-smart
comedy and stories that defied convention

00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:28.222
became a watershed moment in television that
shaped the landscape of sitcoms forever.

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:33.070
Famously a "show about nothing," its episodes
were anything but ordinary, often revolving

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.410
around a single everyday moment like waiting
for a seat at a restaurant or buying a loaf

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.165
of bread.

00:00:38.165 --> 00:00:42.890
However, as quintessential a series as Seinfeld
has become, it didn't start out that way.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:48.440
When it premiered in July of 1989, there were
a couple minor differences in terms of characters,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.000
and it even had a different title.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.680
Airing as The Seinfeld Chronicles, it centered
on New York comedian Jerry Seinfeld and his

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.079
surly friend George Costanza, alongside a
wacky neighbor.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:02.879
It still starred Jerry Seinfeld, Jason Alexander,
and Michael Richards, but the neighbor played

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:05.816
by Richards was named Kessler, not Kramer.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.917
"Oh what are you doing?

00:01:09.182 --> 00:01:10.743
Kessler, it's a tape!"

00:01:10.743 --> 00:01:15.259
What's more, Julia Louis-Dreyfus's character
Elaine was nowhere to be found.

00:01:15.259 --> 00:01:18.890
Even more surprising, the pilot episode was
received negatively by audiences.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.530
Thankfully, the showrunners implemented a
few key changes, simplified the title, and

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:25.703
TV history was made.

00:01:25.703 --> 00:01:31.280
The 1980s might be the all-time best decade
for family sitcoms, seeing the birth of classics

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.503
like Who's the Boss, Growing Pains, Family
Ties, and Full House.

00:01:35.503 --> 00:01:38.896
Smack dab in the middle of these classic series
was The Hogan Family.

00:01:38.896 --> 00:01:43.229
The Sandy Duncan-led comedy followed an aunt
who arrives to take care of her nieces and

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:45.267
nephews after the death of her sister.

00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:50.390
However, that premise isn't actually how the
series started; it was originally titled Valerie

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.909
and starred Valerie Harper in the title role
as a wife and mother of three kids who struggles

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:56.642
to keep her busy life in order.

00:01:56.642 --> 00:02:01.680
After the show's second season, though, Harper
was unceremoniously fired following a contract

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.258
dispute, and her character was killed off-screen.

00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:09.240
So, in came Duncan as Aunt Sandy, and the
show swapped out its title, running for another

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.955
four successful years as The Hogan Family.

00:02:11.955 --> 00:02:16.030
While Harper was forced to watch her own show
go on and find success without her, she would

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:20.675
eventually get the last laugh after the studio
sued her trying to claim she'd quit the series.

00:02:20.675 --> 00:02:26.190
Harper counter-sued in defiance and ultimately
won out when she was awarded $1.4 million

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.916
in lost wages in 1988.

00:02:28.916 --> 00:02:34.275
Arriving on the airwaves in 1975, Saturday
Night Live became an instant classic.

00:02:34.275 --> 00:02:39.300
While SNL certainly wasn't the first successful
sketch comedy show to air, its iconic cast

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.870
of lesser-known raw talent made it decidedly
different from those that had come before.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.019
What's more, the show was far edgier than
its predecessors, breaking down comedic barriers

00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:52.760
and eventually becoming an American cultural
staple that turned nearly its entire cast

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:53.769
into stars.

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:57.489
With such a well-chronicled history, it's
hard to picture that the series ever went

00:02:57.489 --> 00:02:58.385
by a different name.

00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:00.315
But for its first season, it did.

00:03:00.315 --> 00:03:04.799
The reason for the discrepancy was that, ahead
of the premier, SNL's studio learned that

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:08.569
there was already a variety show in development
called Saturday Night Live.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:12.879
To avoid legal problems or confusion among
the audience, the sketch comedy show launched

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:14.900
as NBC's Saturday Night.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:20.099
Conveniently, the other Saturday Night Live
ended after just one year, so NBC rebranded

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:22.750
its late-night series with the title that
we all know now.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.450
And it's a good thing too, because SNL rolls
off the tongue a lot better than NSN.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.930
In the mid-'90s, the Star Trek franchise was
one of the biggest names on TV, with both

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.184
Deep Space Nine and Voyager on the air.

00:03:35.184 --> 00:03:39.760
Later in that decade though, ratings for both
shows began to decline, and it felt like some

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.709
franchise fatigue was setting in.

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:45.939
In response to this downward trend, showrunners
made the decision to wait until both series

00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:47.830
had ended before launching another.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.656
The result was Star Trek: Enterprise.

00:03:49.656 --> 00:03:54.970
Airing its first episodes in the fall of 2001,
the show broke the status quo by being a prequel

00:03:54.970 --> 00:04:00.049
set in the early days of human space exploration
— more than 100 years before Kirk and Spock

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:01.530
went where no one had gone before.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:07.290
"This is Captain Jonathan Archer of the Starship
Enterprise, I don't need to tell you where

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.290
we are."

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:11.890
What some may not remember, however, is that
when the show first began, it broke the norm

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.726
in another way by removing "Star Trek" from
the title.

00:04:14.726 --> 00:04:18.540
Although it's difficult to imagine a major
franchise trying to distance itself from its

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.759
own name, because the Star Trek brand was
fading in popularity, it was a strategic move

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:23.575
at the time.

00:04:23.575 --> 00:04:27.470
Thus, for its first two seasons, the series
was known simply as Enterprise.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.520
It also featured a pop song in the main title
sequence in place of the orchestral themes

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.132
of prior Star Trek shows.

00:04:33.132 --> 00:04:37.990
Unfortunately, these changes did little to
attract a broader audience, and by Season

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.360
3, the show was officially retitled Star Trek:
Enterprise.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.770
With a new showrunner in Season 4, reviews
improved, but it wasn't enough to save it.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.298
The series was sadly given the ax after its
fourth season.

00:04:50.298 --> 00:04:54.880
John Ritter was one of the industry's biggest
sitcom stars thanks to Three's Company and

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.943
its continuation, Three's A Crowd.

00:04:56.943 --> 00:05:01.479
But when those came to an end, he struggled
to find another popular series, starring in

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:04.276
short-lived shows like Hooperman and Hearts
Afire.

00:05:04.276 --> 00:05:09.280
In 2002, though, the actor took the adult
lead in the sitcom 8 Simple Rules, where he

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.720
starred as a middle-aged father and husband
raising three kids.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.590
Future Big Bang Theory alum Kaley Cuoco played
Bridget, Ritter's on-screen daughter.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.600
However, not everyone knows that Bridget was
once a titular character in the series; when

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.200
the show first aired, it had the longer name
8 Simple Rules... for Dating My Teenage Daughter.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.550
In its first season, the show ranked fairly
well in the ratings and seemed poised to become

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:34.870
a hit, so in its second year, the title was
simplified to make it a little less clunky.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.479
"I can't believe I never thought of that.

00:05:36.479 --> 00:05:38.270
I mean I'm always, like, so logical."

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:43.770
Tragically, Ritter's sudden death in 2003
forced a total overhaul, and Season 3 saw

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.753
the show retooled to focus on his character's
father and brother.

00:05:46.753 --> 00:05:51.729
Without Ritter, however, the sitcom just couldn't
stay afloat, and 8 Simple Rules was canceled

00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:54.240
after its third season.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.479
Famous today for featuring a pair of superstars
before they were famous, Two Guys and a Girl

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:02.930
is pretty much exactly what it says in the
title: a sitcom centered on two male college

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:05.060
buddies who are friends with a young woman.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:09.910
Deadpool star Ryan Reynolds played alongside
Richard Ruccolo and Traylor Howard as Berg,

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:11.970
Pete, and Sharon, respectively.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.130
Additionally, Nathan Fillion played Sharon's
boyfriend, Johnny.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:19.400
Though the show was never a major hit, it
did air for four seasons and remains one of

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.650
the most criminally under-appreciated '90s
sitcoms.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:26.870
Similar to 8 Simple Rules, Two Guys and a
Girl initially had a name that was a lot longer

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.320
and tougher to rattle off.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.319
Upon its first release in 1998, the series
was called Two Guys, a Girl, and a Pizza Place,

00:06:33.319 --> 00:06:38.050
and the plot of Season 1 focused on Pete and
Berg as coworkers at a pizza restaurant.

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:41.979
That decision occurred because the show was
actually based on the real-life experiences

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:45.900
of creator Kenny Schwartz, who worked at a
pizza joint while in college.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.082
The title wasn't shortened just to make it
easier to say, either.

00:06:49.082 --> 00:06:54.612
After the show's first season, the pizza place
premise was de-emphasized and eventually ditched altogether.

00:06:54.816 --> 00:07:00.370
This meant removing it from the title, and
thus the show became Two Guys and a Girl.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:04.550
Long before Freaks and Geeks, there was another
iconic teen comedy that made a name for itself

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.900
thanks to its offbeat humor and unique storytelling
style.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:12.970
It might be one of the most underrated '90s
TV shows, and its name was Parker Lewis Can't Lose.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.800
The show followed a popular high schooler
and his oddball friends as they got themselves

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.040
in and out of trouble.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.759
The series starred Corin Nemec as the titular
Parker Lewis, whose penchant for tongue-in-cheek

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:27.479
monologues and fourth-wall-breaking asides
evokes undeniable shades of the John Hughes

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:29.131
classic, Ferris Bueller's Day Off.

00:07:29.131 --> 00:07:33.039
Aside from its strange sense of humor, one
of the things that made the show unique was

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:38.599
its fast-paced filming style, which consisted
of quick cuts, comic book angles, and cartoonish

00:07:38.599 --> 00:07:39.643
sound effects.

00:07:39.643 --> 00:07:43.270
While this aesthetic has helped it become
a cult classic, it may have turned off some

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:48.360
audiences, as many of the show's more avant-garde
elements were pushed aside for its third season.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.550
It was around that time that the series also
saw a name change to the more straightforward,

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:53.956
Parker Lewis.

00:07:53.956 --> 00:07:59.080
There have been countless Batman shows over
the years, from iconic entries like the live-action

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:02.630
series starring Adam West to the legendary
Batman: The Animated Series.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:06.250
In more recent years, the TV exploits of the
Dark Knight have mostly revolved around the

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.333
wider cast of characters in Batman's world.

00:08:08.333 --> 00:08:14.000
With various series fixating on police officer
James Gordon, Batwoman, and the Caped Crusader's

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.780
sidekicks, it was only a matter of time before
Alfred got his own spinoff.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:22.690
In 2019, fans got exactly that with Pennyworth:
The Origin of Batman's Butler.

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:27.240
A prequel to Gotham, the series explores the
early life of Alfred Pennyworth when he worked

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.530
as a James Bond-esque agent running his own
security agency.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:34.190
However, the series didn't start out with
that odd and unwieldy title.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:37.060
Initially, it had the much more pleasing name
of Pennyworth.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:41.520
While it was a simple enough title, by the
show's second season, it seems the network

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.560
was worried that audiences didn't know the
significance of the name and how it is related

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:46.342
to Batman.

00:08:46.342 --> 00:08:50.770
As a result, they changed the name of the
series to include the awkward subtitle for

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:57.260
season 3 in order to make it completely clear
that yes, this is the origin of Batman's butler.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:01.500
No child of the '80s or '90s could forget
Saved by the Bell — possibly the most oddly

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.670
celebrated teen show ever produced.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:09.078
Its esteem is an oddity because it wasn't
that stylish or slick, nor was it particularly good.

00:09:09.078 --> 00:09:13.529
Instead, it was a kitschy, corny, goofball
comedy for the whole family that was meme-worthy

00:09:13.529 --> 00:09:15.686
long before such a concept existed.

00:09:15.686 --> 00:09:21.450
It centered on hip high schooler Zack Morris,
his jock best friend A.C. Slater, nerdy misfit

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:27.157
Screech, trendy Lisa Turtle, straight-A student
Jessie Spano, and cheerleader Kelly Kapowski.

00:09:27.157 --> 00:09:31.760
When the show got its start in 1989, however,
it was something very different and not nearly

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:33.845
as appealing to teens as it later became.

00:09:33.845 --> 00:09:37.721
This was due to the fact that several members
of that cast weren't present yet, and the

00:09:37.721 --> 00:09:39.990
focus of the show wasn't even the kids themselves.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:44.490
Instead, the star of the series was their
teacher, Miss Bliss, played by former child

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:45.820
star Hayley Mills.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.839
Fittingly, the original title of the show
was Good Morning, Miss Bliss, which was how

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:51.946
it was known for its entire first season.

00:09:51.946 --> 00:09:56.610
In fact, Slater, Jesse, and Kelly weren't
yet part of the series, and there were several

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:58.630
others who were there in their place.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:02.790
After 14 episodes, the students became the
center of the stories and Miss Bliss was written

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.160
out of her own show.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.609
"Wow, that's great!

00:10:05.609 --> 00:10:07.000
How did the poor sap take it?"

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.630
After that, the series was renamed Saved by
the Bell, continuing to air with this title

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:13.365
for four seasons.

00:10:13.365 --> 00:10:18.500
In 1960, Hollywood legend Andy Griffith got
his own show: a sitcom about a small-town

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:20.124
sheriff named Andy Taylor.

00:10:20.124 --> 00:10:25.850
Future Three's Company star Don Knotts co-starred
alongside him as Barney Fife, a bumbling goofball,

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.840
while a young Ron Howard played Taylor's son
Opie.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:33.329
The Andy Griffith Show was a smash hit, becoming
one of the biggest shows of its era.

00:10:33.329 --> 00:10:37.420
With ratings to compete with powerhouse series
like Bonanza and Bewitched, it ultimately

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.550
ran for more than a decade… depending on
how you look at it.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:44.610
The exact length of time the show aired is
somewhat up for debate; following Andy Griffith's

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:48.290
exit after Season 8, the series got a new
name: Mayberry, R.F.D.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:53.250
While the change is sometimes considered the
beginning of a new series, the fact that most

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.760
of the supporting cast from the previous year
returned means it's often viewed as a continuation

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.082
of the original show.

00:10:59.082 --> 00:11:03.220
Named for the fictional town the series was
set in, Mayberry, R.F.D.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:06.464
centered on Ken Berry's character, town councilman
Sam Jones.

00:11:06.464 --> 00:11:10.531
While many other recurring characters returned
throughout the show's run, Griffith, along

00:11:10.531 --> 00:11:15.760
with his co-stars Knotts and Howard, was noticeably
absent aside from the occasional guest appearance.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.029
Though the series' shift away from Andy Taylor
could have killed the show, Mayberry, R.F.D.

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:21.804
lived on for another three seasons.

